// The `prelude.supervise` sub-module: the retry mechanism, split from the failure policy. A provider
// catches exactly one request — `interrupted` — and either re-runs the rest of the block or hands the
// signal back as a value. Which failures become an `interrupted` is decided by a user-written
// "converter": an ordinary handler between the provider and the body.
//
//   use supervise.exponential(max_attempts = 5)
//   use handler {
//     request prelude.throw(error: fetch_error | auth_error) -> never {
//       match (error) {
//         case fetch_error(_) -> { supervise.interrupted(failure = error) }
//         case auth_error(_)  -> { prelude.throw(error = error) }
//       }
//     }
//   }
//
// Every loop here is a `forever` rather than self-recursion: a delegation pushes a durable frame that
// lives until the call returns, so a recursive loop's depth is its iteration count and none of it is
// reclaimed while it runs. A `forever` is flat — each finished iteration's frames are reclaimed and the
// loop's own `var`s are the only surviving state. The rule generalizes to anything that iterates
// without a known-small bound.
//
// `replay_after` / `give_up` are internal machinery, module members only because Katari has no
// module-private declarations.

@"The supervision signal — the one request a `supervise` provider catches, and the only
performable request this module exports. A converter performs it to ask that the enclosing block be re-run,
carrying the failure it chose to represent as @failure@. `-> never`: performing it transfers control to
the provider, which applies its delay policy and re-runs, and does not return. A provider installed inside the program is what serves it; the
runtime never machine-answers `interrupted`."
request interrupted[F](@"The converter-chosen value standing for the underlying failure (re-raised as `throw[F]` when a bounded provider exhausts)." failure: F) -> never

@"A panic reached a converter — an interrupted call under the at-most-once rule, or a defect. It
carries only @message@, because that is all a panic carries. It gives `signal_panics` a value to signal
with, distinguishable from a throw payload of the same type when both converters stack."
data panicked(@"The panic message." message: string)

@"Turn every `throw[T]` from the rest of the block into the supervision signal, carrying the thrown
payload unchanged as the failure, so `signal_throws[my_error]` gives a `signalled[my_error]`. It
converts totally, since a throw handler admits everything the block can throw; selection happens
afterwards, in the `match` over the signal, with a `prelude.throw` arm for what should keep flying.
Written `use supervise.signal_throws[my_error]()`."
agent signal_throws[T, R, effect E](
  @"The rest of the block (bound by `use`)." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, prelude.throw[T], interrupted[T]},
) -> R with {...E, interrupted[T]} {
  use handler {
    request prelude.throw(error: T) -> never { interrupted(failure = error) }
  }
  continuation(value = null)
}

@"Turn every panic from the rest of the block into the supervision signal, carrying `panicked(message)`
— which is how a runtime restart becomes a value the program can answer, since an external call the
runtime interrupted panics the frame that held it.

@F@ is the residual — what else may signal in this block — and it is not inferred, so spell it:
`use supervise.signal_panics[never]()` when this is the only converter, `[T]` when it wraps a
`signal_throws[T]` and the block's one failure type is meant to be `T | panicked`."
agent signal_panics[F, R, effect E](
  @"The rest of the block (bound by `use`)." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, interrupted[panicked | F]},
) -> R with {...E, interrupted[panicked | F]} {
  use handler {
    request panic(msg: string) { interrupted(failure = panicked(message = msg)) }
  }
  continuation(value = null)
}

@"One run that completed with @value@ — it did not signal."
data ran[R](@"The completed run's result." value: R)

@"One run that performed `interrupted`, carrying the @failure@ its converter chose."
data signalled[F](@"The failure the run's `interrupted` carried." failure: F)

@"Run @task@ once and answer with which happened — `ran(value)` or `signalled(failure)`. Where the
replaying providers answer an `interrupted` by re-running the block, this one hands the failure back as
a value and never re-enters the task.

That is the shape for a block a re-run would not fit: an external call the runtime interrupted is not
re-run (the at-most-once rule), and a body that already had effects would repeat them. @F@ is inferred
from @task@ and preserved into `signalled`; `interrupted[F]` sits in @task@'s overwrite row because
this agent discharges it. It catches only the supervision signal, leaving `throw` and `panic` to the
converter."
agent once[R, F, effect E](
  @"The work to run once, with `interrupted` served for it. A converter installed inside it (or around this call) decides which throws and panics become the signal." task: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, interrupted[F]},
) -> ran[R] | signalled[F] with E {
  use handler {
    request interrupted(failure: F) -> never { break signalled(failure = failure) }
  }
  ran(value = task(value = null))
}

@"Replay the rest of the block on every `interrupted`, unbounded and with NO artificial delay: it
re-runs at once, so the cadence is set by whatever the converter does before it signals — a converter
that parks on a human-in-the-loop escalation gives the attended / re-auth loop, human-paced with no
polling. It never re-raises and never returns on its own; a converter that rethrows instead of
signalling is how a failure leaves this loop. Written `use supervise.immediate()`."
agent immediate[R, F, effect E](
  @"The rest of the block (bound by `use`)." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, interrupted[F]},
) -> R with E {
  forever {
    match (once(task = continuation)) {
      case ran(value => value) -> { break value }
      case signalled(_) -> { null }
    }
  }
}

@"Replay the rest of the block on every `interrupted`, unbounded, with exponential backoff capped at
@max_delay_milliseconds@ — for daemons that must stay up across transient failures. It never re-raises
and never returns on its own, and the durable state stays flat across any number of replays. Every
parameter is defaulted, so the ordinary form is the bare `use supervise.forever()`.

Supervising a region: a fiber's panic is delivered as the typed `region.crashed` event rather than
unwinding past `region.watch`, so a resident whose work runs in fibers performs `interrupted` from its
`region.crashed` clause, installed inside this scope. A re-run rebuilds what the block installs; state
owned by a handler above this `use` survives every attempt."
agent forever[R, F, effect E](
  @"The delay before the first re-run, in milliseconds." initial_delay_milliseconds: number ?= 1000.0,
  @"The delay multiplier (e.g. 2 doubles the delay on each re-run)." factor: number ?= 2.0,
  @"The backoff ceiling, in milliseconds; the delay never grows past it." max_delay_milliseconds: number ?= 900000.0,
  @"The rest of the block (bound by `use`)." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, interrupted[F]},
) -> R with E | io {
  forever (var delay = initial_delay_milliseconds) {
    match (once(task = continuation)) {
      case ran(value => value) -> { break value }
      case signalled(_) -> {
        time.sleep(milliseconds = delay)
        next with { delay = math.min(left = delay * factor, right = max_delay_milliseconds) }
      }
    }
  }
}

@"A bounded-retry decision: keep going after sleeping the current delay."
data replay_after()

@"The exhausted arm of a bounded-retry decision: no attempts remain, so the provider re-raises the failure."
data give_up()

@"Replay the rest of the block on `interrupted` with exponential backoff, up to @max_attempts@ times.
On failure number n (1-based) with n < @max_attempts@ it durably sleeps @initial_delay_milliseconds@ *
@factor@^(n-1), then re-runs; when the budget is spent it re-raises that failure as a typed `throw[F]`,
so exhaustion keeps the error typed end to end and never re-panics. The cadence is defaulted and
@max_attempts@ is not, so the ordinary form is `use supervise.exponential(max_attempts = 5)`."
agent exponential[R, F, effect E](
  @"The delay before the first re-run, in milliseconds." initial_delay_milliseconds: number ?= 1000.0,
  @"The delay multiplier (e.g. 2 doubles the delay on each re-run)." factor: number ?= 2.0,
  @"The total run budget, not a retry count: the @max_attempts@-th `interrupted` re-raises instead of re-running. No default — how many tries is the caller's judgement." max_attempts: number,
  @"The rest of the block (bound by `use`)." continuation: agent (value: null) -> R with {...E, interrupted[F]},
) -> R with E | io | prelude.throw[F] {
  forever (var attempt = 0, var delay = initial_delay_milliseconds) {
    match (once(task = continuation)) {
      case ran(value => value) -> { break value }
      case signalled(failure => failure) -> {
        // The one comparison boundary: classify the running failure count against the budget into a
        // sum once, then dispatch on the sum so no later code re-tests the counter.
        let verdict = if (attempt + 1 < max_attempts) { replay_after() } else { give_up() }
        match (verdict) {
          case replay_after(_) -> {
            time.sleep(milliseconds = delay)
            next with { attempt = attempt + 1, delay = delay * factor }
          }
          case give_up(_) -> { prelude.throw(error = failure) }
        }
      }
    }
  }
}
